WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.800
Material samples brought back

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.644
from objects in space can shed light

00:00:10.644 --> 00:00:13.360
on billions of years of
our solar system's history,

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.490
including details about the
origin of life here on Earth.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.640
A piece of an asteroid is a piece

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.520
of the earliest beginnings
of the solar system.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.910
4.6 billion years ago, when
our solar system formed,

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:33.060
initially there was just gas
and dust swirling around and

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.900
that coalesced into planetesimals,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.420
and those things crashed into
each other and got bigger

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.780
and some became planets,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.990
and those that did not
remained as asteroids.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:50.990
And they're like little
snapshots of Earth's beginnings.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.830
But picking up rocks from the surface

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.500
of an asteroid or planetary satellite

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.870
isn't as easy as it sounds.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.810
It requires voyages of
millions of miles to bodies

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.990
that have never been seen up close.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:03.540
Probes need to land on surfaces

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.340
with weak gravitational pulls
where the surface terrain

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.040
is completely unknown, gather samples,

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.713
and then make the long trip back.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.360
And while behemoths like NASA
and Space X grab headlines,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.140
it's a relatively young space agency,

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.720
the Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency,

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.130
better known as JAXA,

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.080
that is taking the giant leap necessary

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.033
to bring the mysteries of
space back down to Earth.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.220
If we want to find out about
the origin of our solar system,

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.420
or the origin of life itself,
we need to study materials

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.610
that were around 4.6 billion years ago

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.770
when the solar system was born.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.850
Meteorites actually fit that bill.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.380
And luckily the earth is lousy with them.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.250
Meteorites make impacts with
our planet all the time.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.683
There's just one problem.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.020
JAXA itself was officially
founded in 2003,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.120
when three different
national space agencies

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.700
were merged into one.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.540
Prior agencies in Japan had excelled

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.710
in infrared and x-ray astronomy
and the design and launch

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.923
of communication satellites and rockets.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.320
JAXA continued those efforts

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.250
as it began devoting money and research

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.433
into sample return.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:53.440
With the need for relatively
untouched materials

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:54.730
from space clear,

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.760
planning for the world's
first sample return

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.403
from an asteroid began in 1995.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.830
On May 9th, 2003, the
robotic spacecraft Hayabusa,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.530
meaning falcon in English,

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:10.330
launched with its path set
for the asteroid Itokawa,

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.900
a near earth asteroid located
between Mars and Earth.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.560
Using its four ion engines

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.650
for the 1,200,000 mile-plus journey,

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:21.540
Hayabusa made contact with
Itokawa more than two years

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.180
after its launch, in September of 2005.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.500
That is a special challenge

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:31.290
because asteroids are
not as large as planets

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.570
and they don't have the gravity

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.170
to allow you to just land.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:40.370
So you have to figure out
how to land and stick there,

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.080
and then have your instrument, your drill,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.913
or your collector, operate
in those conditions.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.190
Not everything went according to plan.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.050
Weeks were spent trying and failing

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.410
to launch Hayabusa's designated probe,

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.300
and there was a fuel leak
from its chemical engine.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.310
Part of the difficulty
with the Hayabusa mission

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.620
was with the size of Itokawa itself.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.320
Only 1100 feet in diameter,
it's gravitational pull is weak.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.310
So Hayabusa had to maintain
heliocentric orbit,

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.370
meaning the craft would
track the asteroid's path

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.580
around the sun.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.230
In addition, the distance
from Earth required Hayabusa

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.263
to be fully autonomous, thanks
to the communication delay.

00:04:40.817 --> 00:04:41.750
The probe was finally able

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.740
to touch down and attempt
a sample retrieval

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.563
on Itokawa for 30 minutes.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.550
As Hayabusa returned, no one knew

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.370
whether the sample collection
had been a success or not,

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.190
but upon its arrival to
earth in June of 2010,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.450
less than one milligram
of dust from Itokawa

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.600
was discovered in its collection module,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.310
a truly tiny specimen, but nevertheless,

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.150
the first successful asteroid
sample return in history.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.823
One of the conclusions
was that the meteorite,

00:05:37.823 --> 00:05:40.920
Itokawa, that was studied, was consistent

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.270
with stony meteorites that
we have found on the earth.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.420
But we couldn't really conclude that

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:52.420
until we could get an actual
sample from the asteroid,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.813
bring it back, and make
a direct comparison.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.400
JAXA would expand on this flawed,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.090
but successful mission, with
the launch of Hayabusa 2

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:14.100
to the asteroid Ryugu in 2014.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.730
The second asteroid sample
retrieval mission was notable,

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.460
not only for its more advanced
machinery and distance

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.470
of 9 million miles, but
for its destination.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.790
Unlike Itokawa's inert collection
of boulder sized rocks,

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.090
Ryugu is carbonaceous,
meaning it contains some

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.380
of the solar systems most
pristine, untouched materials;

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.600
minerals, ice, and organic materials.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:39.600
The carbonaceous ones are
of great interest to us,

00:06:39.987 --> 00:06:43.440
especially in this
recent Hayabusa 2 mission

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.040
because they contain
remnant organic matter

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.820
and also, water,

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.520
that may have been the source

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.707
of organic matter to evolve life,

00:06:54.707 --> 00:06:58.363
and the source of water
for the oceans on earth.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:03.240
After it's approach to Ryugu,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.010
Hayabusa 2 deployed a
series of three small rovers

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.980
that, due to the asteroid's low gravity,

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.450
made a series of hops
rather than roll on wheels

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:11.853
to survey its surface.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.540
In order to retrieve a
subsurface sample free

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:16.980
from the weathering of space,

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.890
the craft needed to fire a projectile

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:20.580
into the surface of the asteroid,

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.333
and in another first, created
a man-made crater in Ryugu.

00:07:25.797 --> 00:07:27.610
Hayabusa 2 was then able to descend

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.883
and conduct subsurface sampling
from the site of the blast.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:33.370
Unlike the first mission,

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.450
the second was able to
collect more than five grams

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:37.610
of material from the surface of Ryugu.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.720
Now the largest asteroid
sample ever recovered.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.910
Originally, we expected to
have the samples greater

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:49.910
than .1 gram, but ended up with 4.5 grams,

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.670
which is more than 50 times of the grams.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.440
The next surprise was that,

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.780
there is a infrared microscope,

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.263
found water bearing minerals.

00:08:01.571 --> 00:08:05.060
The Hayabusa 2 mission, sampling Ryugu,

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.760
has yet to yield information

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.820
because the samples came back in 2020,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:15.000
they've been distributed
to labs all over the world

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.620
and we'll soon get information from them

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:22.610
on the origin of water,
potentially, from meteorites,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:27.500
as well as the nature of
carbonaceous material.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:31.810
JAXA's next sample return mission

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.820
is set to be its most
ambitious and illuminating yet.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:36.490
And it's to an area of the solar system

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.980
that's particularly popular at the moment.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:42.200
The MMX or Martian Moons
Exploration mission

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:46.030
is slated to visit to have
Mars' moons - Phobos and Deimos,

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:48.133
with a launch plan for 2024.

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:51.290
MMX will visit and survey both moons

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:52.680
with the hope of collecting a sample

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.423
from Phobos in mid 2025, and
a return to earth in 2029.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.620
MMX seeks to find whether
Phobos and Deimos are asteroids

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.070
that became caught in
Mars' orbit long ago,

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.930
or if they were fragments that coalesced

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.160
after a major impact with the red planet,

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:10.640
as scientists more often suspect.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.100
There are also implications
for the origins of life

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.203
in our solar system.

00:09:41.209 --> 00:09:43.430
MMX will be humanity's first opportunity

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:46.330
at bringing back samples
from other planetary systems

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:48.600
and JAXA hopes that the round trip visit

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:50.690
will reveal important
information that can be used

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.240
on future manned missions
to the martian sphere,

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:56.233
including whether Phobos can
be used as an orbital base.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.140
On MMX, part of the sampling will be done

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:07.450
with the help of US-based
Honeybee Robotics,

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.400
meet PlanetVac, the planetary
vacuum sucks up regolith,

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.810
another name for the dirt lying

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:15.970
on top of the bedrock of space bodies,

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:18.880
and collects it to either be
analyzed by on-board tools

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:21.520
or stored away for return to earth.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.200
The PlanetVac takes the
place of a leg on a lander

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:25.500
and uses compressed gas

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.470
that's usually already on board the craft,

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:29.023
to pressurize fuel tanks.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.700
MMX is still currently
in the development stage

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.013
and JAXA says the cost will
be around $419 million.

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:38.600
If all goes according to plan,

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.600
JAXA can further cement its track record

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.313
as the world's premier
experts at sample return.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.790
JAXA sample return missions
will, over the coming decades,

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.410
help prepare other space
agencies for manned missions

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:52.670
to various types of environments in space,

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:53.890
and as research continues

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:55.730
on the samples already brought back,

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:58.040
help to solve some of the
mysteries that still exist

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.333
about the formation of life on earth.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:27.573
The world over,

00:11:27.573 --> 00:11:30.340
smaller space agencies
have found crucial niches

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.630
that don't require the
massive funding of NASA,

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:34.273
Blue Origin, or Space X.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:38.050
In this way, JAXA is more
than holding their own

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:40.867
with our worlds' space heavyweights.

